      "stale_hits": [],
      "global_stale_hits": [],
      "answer": "TL;DR: The Council room interferes with the fight using arcane traps, illusions, and environmental hazards\u2014paralysis, force damage, prone effects, disorientation, and guard-triggering alarms.\n\nInside the Council Chambers, the environment actively disrupts players in several ways. Arcane trap bursts can temporarily paralyze players and deal 2d6 force damage (Claims 1\u20132). These traps may specifically target players who lack identifying amulets, while guards are exempt due to their friendly-identification amulets (Claims 7\u20139).\n\nThe room also creates physical hazards: falling debris forces players to dodge, and effects can throw them prone (Claims 3\u20134). On top of that, illusions play a major role\u2014illusory walls and spells disorient players, and the Wolf\u2019s illusory duplicates further confuse positioning and targeting (Claims 5\u20136).\n\nSecurity escalates the chaos: rune alarms can alert nearby guards, who then actively block exits, while approaching reinforcements may be hinted at by echoing footsteps (Claims 10\u201312). Subtle cues like changes in the city\u2019s constant mechanical hum can signal significant events mid-fight (Claims 13\u201315).\n\nKey Attributes:\n- Defenses: Arcane traps (paralysis, force damage), illusion effects, rune alarms  \n- Security System: Amulet-based ally recognition; guards immune to traps  \n- Environmental Hazards: Falling debris, prone effects  \n- Sensory Signals: Echoing footsteps, shifting mechanical hum",
      "embedding_similarity": null
    },
